From: luckfox-linux <build@localhost>
Subject: [PATCH] rknpu: hold a reference to the dma-buf it keeps mapped

RKNPU_MEM_CREATE with args.handle == 0 has the driver allocate the
buffer itself, and then hand its only reference to userspace:

	dmabuf = rk_dma_heap_buffer_alloc(rknpu_dev->heap, args.size, ...);
	rknpu_obj->dmabuf = dmabuf;
	rknpu_obj->owner = 1;

	fd = dma_buf_fd(dmabuf, O_CLOEXEC | O_RDWR);

dma_buf_fd() installs the file without taking a reference of its own;
the fd takes over the one the allocation returned. From there the
driver attaches to the buffer, dma_buf_vmap()s it when the caller
asked for RKNPU_MEM_KERNEL_MAPPING, and keeps both the attachment and
the kernel mapping in the rknpu_mem_object -- while holding nothing
that keeps the buffer alive. Both teardown paths agree with the
create path and skip the put when ->owner is set.

That works only for as long as userspace calls RKNPU_MEM_DESTROY, or
closes /dev/rknpu, before the last fd and mapping go:
rknpu_mem_destroy_ioctl() and rknpu_release() vunmap and detach first,
so the buffer is still alive when they touch it. Nothing enforces that
order. A process that is killed never gets to run the runtime's
teardown, and do_exit() tears the address space down in exit_mm()
before it closes the fd table in exit_files(), so a buffer the runtime
mmap()ed and whose fd it had already closed loses its last reference
while the driver still has a kernel mapping on it. Ctrl-C during
inference is enough:

  Internal error: Oops - BUG: 0 [#1] THUMB2
  CPU: 0 PID: 387 Comm: yolo_detect Not tainted 6.6.89+ #1
  PC is at dma_buf_release+0xa/0x58
  ...
   dma_buf_release from __dentry_kill+0x73/0xb6
   __dentry_kill from dput+0x2f/0x34
   dput from __fput+0x11f/0x178
   __fput from task_work_run+0x49/0x60
   task_work_run from do_exit+0x235/0x570
  Code: 6e44 b33c 6963 b103 (de02) 6d63

that being BUG_ON(dmabuf->vmapping_counter) at the top of
dma_buf_release(), with the counter at 1 and the attachment list still
non-empty. Had the BUG_ON not caught it, rknpu_release() would have
gone on to vunmap and detach a freed dma_buf.

So take a reference in the owner case too, immediately after the fd is
installed, and drop it unconditionally wherever the object is torn
down. The count is then simply one per rknpu_mem_object, however the
buffer was obtained, and the buffer outlives the driver's mapping of it
no matter how the process ends. This also repairs the error path:
dma_buf_attach() or dma_buf_map_attachment() failing after the fd was
installed used to free the buffer out from under an fd userspace had
already been given.

->owner no longer decides anything, but it is left in place and still
set. It is documented in rknpu_mem.h as "is this memory internally
allocated", and deleting a member of a vendor struct is churn whose
only effect here would be to make this patch likelier to stop applying.

While in the error path, only vunmap if something was in fact vmapped.
Without RKNPU_MEM_KERNEL_MAPPING the iosys_map is still zeroed, and
dma_buf_vunmap() opens with BUG_ON(iosys_map_is_null(&dmabuf->vmap_ptr)),
so a copy_to_user() that faults is a second way for userspace to bring
the kernel down.

Signed-off-by: luckfox-linux <build@localhost>
---
 drivers/rknpu/rknpu_drv.c |  3 +--
 drivers/rknpu/rknpu_mem.c | 23 +++++++++++++++--------
 2 files changed, 16 insertions(+), 10 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/rknpu/rknpu_drv.c b/drivers/rknpu/rknpu_drv.c
index a0af79b..ded473a 100644
--- a/drivers/rknpu/rknpu_drv.c
+++ b/drivers/rknpu/rknpu_drv.c
@@ -577,8 +577,7 @@ static int rknpu_release(struct inode *inode, struct file *file)
 					 DMA_BIDIRECTIONAL);
 		dma_buf_detach(entry->dmabuf, entry->attachment);
 
-		if (!entry->owner)
-			dma_buf_put(entry->dmabuf);
+		dma_buf_put(entry->dmabuf);
 
 		list_del(&entry->head);
 		kfree(entry);
diff --git a/drivers/rknpu/rknpu_mem.c b/drivers/rknpu/rknpu_mem.c
index 84ec2a7..3273f5e 100644
--- a/drivers/rknpu/rknpu_mem.c
+++ b/drivers/rknpu/rknpu_mem.c
@@ -132,6 +132,15 @@ int rknpu_mem_create_ioctl(struct rknpu_device *rknpu_dev, struct file *file,
 			ret = -EFAULT;
 			goto err_free_dma_buf;
 		}
+
+		/*
+		 * dma_buf_fd() installs the file without taking a reference:
+		 * the fd has taken over the one rk_dma_heap_buffer_alloc()
+		 * returned. Take our own, so the attachment and the kernel
+		 * mapping made below keep the buffer alive for as long as
+		 * this rknpu_mem_object does.
+		 */
+		get_dma_buf(dmabuf);
 	}
 
 	attachment = dma_buf_attach(dmabuf, rknpu_dev->dev);
@@ -195,18 +204,17 @@ int rknpu_mem_create_ioctl(struct rknpu_device *rknpu_dev, struct file *file,
 	return 0;
 
 err_unmap_kv_addr:
-	dma_buf_vunmap(rknpu_obj->dmabuf, &map);
-	rknpu_obj->kv_addr = NULL;
+	if (rknpu_obj->kv_addr) {
+		dma_buf_vunmap(rknpu_obj->dmabuf, &map);
+		rknpu_obj->kv_addr = NULL;
+	}
 
 err_detach_dma_buf:
 	dma_buf_unmap_attachment(attachment, table, DMA_BIDIRECTIONAL);
 	dma_buf_detach(dmabuf, attachment);
 
 err_free_dma_buf:
-	if (rknpu_obj->owner)
-		rk_dma_heap_buffer_free(dmabuf);
-	else
-		dma_buf_put(dmabuf);
+	dma_buf_put(dmabuf);
 
 err_free_obj:
 	kfree(rknpu_obj);
@@ -269,8 +277,7 @@ int rknpu_mem_destroy_ioctl(struct rknpu_device *rknpu_dev, struct file *file,
 					 DMA_BIDIRECTIONAL);
 		dma_buf_detach(rknpu_obj->dmabuf, rknpu_obj->attachment);
 
-		if (!rknpu_obj->owner)
-			dma_buf_put(rknpu_obj->dmabuf);
+		dma_buf_put(rknpu_obj->dmabuf);
 
 		kfree(rknpu_obj);
 	}
